Audit Immersion Questions — Forced Implementation Checklist

The forcing rule
• The AI runs every question in this document against every tych before delivering output.
• Every question must pass. One failure means the scene fails.
• When a question fails, fix the scene. Never weaken the question.
• No skipping. No leniency. No redos counted as passes.
1. Plausible unfiltered physical reality
• How would this look IRL? Would a real camera capture exactly this?
• What is holding this body's weight right now? Can it credibly carry it?
• Is anything floating, clipping, unsupported, or effortless that real life would never allow?
• If the shrunk person were removed entirely, would the scene still be a complete, coherent real place?
• Would a stranger looking at this for one second believe it happened, or believe it was made?
• Is anything here existing only to make the image work instead of the world work?
• Is the emotion proportional to what is actually happening, or is it bigger than the moment earns?
• Does this face show real asymmetry — different brow heights, uneven mouth, eyes not locked to the nose vector — or is it a mask?
• Does this hand have five fingers doing five different believable things, or is it a prop hand?
• What is this person's weight doing to the surface beneath them? Is there dent, spread, or give, or is the surface ignoring them?
• Would the sound of this moment — breath, fabric, contact — match what is shown?
2. 1:1 weighted normal-sized reality
• Is the world full-scale normal reality built for normal-sized people?
• Is every object at its true real-world size?
• Does anything rescale, shrink, or rearrange to accommodate the small human?
• Does every contact show both sides reacting — compression, fold, shift, pressure?
• Would this fabric behave this way under this force on a real body?
• Would this object at this exact size function the way it is used, or is it the right size doing the wrong job?
• If the camera moved to the other side, would the body, scale, and physics still be identical?
• Can every effect be traced to a visible cause, and every cause to a visible effect?
3. Sizes and measurements
• Is every measurement written in exact units with an exact number?
• Did any sentence say "small," "tiny," "large," or any word a model could interpret freely?
• Does every size statement name the unit and the number, so there is exactly one possible reading?
• Is the scale anchor stated — the object or body this size is measured against — or is the size floating in space?
• Does this measurement match the one stated two panels ago, to the exact unit?
• If two sizes are compared, is the comparison exact (half, twice, one-third) or vague (smaller, bigger)?
• Is there any word here doing the job of a measurement that only a measurement can do?
• Is this proportion stated as a ratio or fraction of a known whole?
• Does this limb's length trace back to the locked body measurements, or was a number invented on the spot?
• Would a model reading this know precisely how tall, how wide, and how far — or would it have to guess?
4. Shrinking integrity — the adult stays adult
• Is the shrinking instant and already finished? Is there any beam, glow, or mid-change frame suggesting it is still happening?
• Are there lingering visual effects implying the shrink is ongoing — shimmer, distortion, blur of transition?
• Is this person the same adult at the new size — same age, same anatomy, same head?
• Would anyone reading this figure see a small adult, or would they see a child?
• Did every measurement survive the size change, scaled by exactly one factor?
• Is the size stated as an exact adult-scale number — never framed as baby-sized, child-sized, or toddler-sized?
• Does anything about this figure — head ratio, limb length, softness — borrow from a child's body instead of the adult's?
• If compared to their full-size self, would every part match part-for-part, only smaller?
• Did identity survive — same face, same body, same scars, same proportions?
• Is the clothing consistent with a body that shrank in it, or did the outfit change size on its own logic?
5. Doll-like rendering — kill checks
• Does this hair have real weight, real strands, real imperfection — or is it molded, helmet-like, one solid piece?
• Does this skin show real texture — pores, fine lines, variation in tone — or is it smooth, poreless, airbrushed like vinyl?
• Are these eyes real — depth, moisture, reflection — or flat, painted, glass-doll eyes?
• Are these hands real hands — knuckles, tendons, varied fingers — or simplified doll appendages?
• Is there any seam, joint line, plastic sheen, or manufactured edge anywhere on this body?
• Does this body have real soft tissue — give, fold, compression under contact — or rigid, molded, unchanging forms?
• Would this figure fool no one into thinking "toy"? Is there anything about it a doll collector would recognize?
• If this person blinked, breathed, or shifted weight, would the image still hold — or is it frozen in a way only an object can be?
• Is the skin, hair, and soft tissue detailed? Were doll hair and doll skin avoided?
6. Stumpy proportions and body integrity
• Is every limb its full adult length, scaled by one factor — or has anything been shortened, stubbed, or compressed?
• Do the arms reach where real arms reach, and the legs carry where real legs carry?
• Is the torso its full length, or has it been compacted to fit the frame?
• Does this body still measure eight heads tall with every segment in its locked proportion?
• Is this body part the exact length and width it was at full size, scaled by one factor — or did a limb quietly change?
• Does every visible body part belong to a fully visible whole person?
• Is there a second person, limb, or shadow in frame that nobody asked for?
• Does every hand have one assigned action in one assigned place?
7. Camera integrity
• Is the camera an ordinary camera that changes viewpoint only?
• Has any camera angle foreshortened a limb into looking shorter than it is — and does the true length still read?
• Does a low or high angle make this person look bigger or smaller than their stated size?
• Is any body part closer to the camera in a way that exaggerates it — head, hand, foot looming larger than its true size?
• If this exact body were photographed from the opposite side, would it still measure the same in every dimension?
• Is the camera showing the body, or is the camera reshaping the body?
• Is the light hitting this body from the same direction as everything else in the scene?
• Are the lighting and shadows true-to-life?
• Is there any forced perspective, miniature look, or cheat that makes the world appear smaller than it is?
• Does the camera stay unacknowledged? Does anyone pose, present, or look into it?
8. Continuity and causality across tychs
• What changed between this panel and the last one? Was a cause shown for every single change?
• Do identity, size, position, lighting, world state, and physical consequences carry causally from panel to panel with no silent reset?
• Does the background continue the same room, same time of day, same weather as the last panel — or did the world quietly reset?
• Does the expression match what this person knows and feels in this moment, or is it generic?
• Is there emotional inertia — do moods carry forward and shift only through depicted causes?
• If this person stood up right now, would their balance, joint angles, and muscle tension actually allow it?
• Would a real person move, balance, or reach this way — or is the pose serving the camera?
9. Handling and contact physics
• Is the handling torso-and-body gripping, with the handler controlling the center of mass through the trunk?
• Does the small body hang, dangle, brace, or twist with real weight and real resistance against the grip?
• Is anyone standing in an open palm, pinched by fingertips, or carried passively like cargo in a bowl?
• Does the handler's side show effort — tendons, shifted stance, counterbalance?
• Is contact asymmetrical and awkward, following the struggle — never neat, never symmetrical, never posed?
10. Language with no ambiguity
• Could this sentence be read two ways? If so, which way will the model pick?
• Could "it," "they," or "this" point to more than one thing in this sentence?
• Is the sequence of actions in an order that can only be read one way — left to right, cause to effect?
• Is there any fact here that contradicts any other fact in the scene?
• If every adjective were deleted, would the measurements alone still build the same figure?
• Does this description pin down the body, or does it leave the body to the model's imagination?
• Does any term or wording confuse the current sizes of anything?
11. Final gate
• Run every question above against every tych.
• Every question passes, or the scene does not ship.
• When a question fails, fix the scene. Never weaken the question.
